   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "Let \\( g \\) be the function defined by \\( g(x) = \\int_{1}^{x} \\left(-t(t + 3)(t - 5)\\right) \\, dt \\) for \\( -1 < x < 7 \\). Find the value of \\( x \\) at which \\( g \\) has a relative maximum."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 5
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, \\( g'(x) = -x(x + 3)(x - 5) \\) for \\( -1 < x < 7 \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Multiply",
            "x",
            [
               "Add",
               "x",
               -5
            ],
            [
               "Add",
               [
                  "Negate",
                  "x"
               ],
               -3
            ]
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Set \\( g'(x) = 0 \\) and factor: \\( -x(x + 3)(x - 5) = 0 \\), so \\( x = -3, 0, 5 \\).",
         "mathjson": 5
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Only \\( x = 0 \\) and \\( x = 5 \\) lie in \\( -1 < x < 7 \\); \\( x = -3 \\) is outside the interval on which \\( g \\) is defined, so it is not a critical point of \\( g \\).",
         "mathjson": 5
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "\\( g' \\) changes from positive to negative at \\( x = 5 \\) and from negative to positive at \\( x = 0 \\), so \\( g \\) has its relative maximum at \\( x = 5 \\).",
         "mathjson": 5
      }
   ],
